findings.body:
Inside the recurring-cancellation branch the code does:

  bookingToDelete.user.credentials
    .filter((credential) => credential.type.endsWith("_calendar"))
    .forEach(async (credential) => {
      const calendar = await getCalendar(credential);
      for (const updBooking of updatedBookings) {
        ...
        const deletedEvent = await calendar?.deleteEvent(uid, evt, externalCalendarId);
        apiDeletes.push(deletedEvent);
      }
    });

`forEach` does not await its async callback, so the outer flow continues and `Promise.all(prismaPromises.concat(apiDeletes))` runs before the per-credential async loops have pushed their `deletedEvent` Promises (or worse, after a partial race). For recurring bookings this means many calendar deletion calls are effectively dropped or not waited for, leaving the previous time slot still booked on the user's external calendar. Use `for...of` (matching the non-recurring branch a few lines below) or `Promise.all(calendarCredentials.map(...))`.

`getCalendarCredentials` is still declared as a synchronous function but internally calls the now-async `getCalendar(credential)` without `await`:

  const calendar = getCalendar(credential);
  return app.variant === "calendar" ? [{ integration: app, credential, calendar }] : [];

So each returned entry's `calendar` field is now `Promise<Calendar | null>` rather than `Calendar | null`. The only caller shown in the diff (`getConnectedCalendars`) was patched to do `const calendar = await item.calendar;`, but the function itself remains a synchronous leaky abstraction. Any other consumer of `getCalendarCredentials` will silently receive a Promise where it expects a Calendar and will call `.deleteEvent` / `.listCalendars` / `.createEvent` on the Promise object. The function should either be made `async` and awaited inside, or the `getCalendar` call should be awaited before constructing the result object.
